WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.300
A Christmas Carol adaptations range from the
delightful to the derivative.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.740
Just how do the many iterations of Dickens'
classic tale of redemption stack up?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.580
From the total disasters to the heartfelt
classics...

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.060
"Humbug."

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.420
...here are A Christmas Carol adaptations
ranked from worst to best.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.980
Have you ever thought to yourself that A Christmas
Carol could use some slapstick animal gags

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.540
to undercut all the Victorian gloom?

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.060
How about a sleeker, younger Ebenezer Scrooge
who's given a second chance at love?

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.580
Perhaps a love song performed by Kate Winslet?

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.140
No?

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.640
Well, that's too bad, because 2001's Christmas
Carol: The Movie is stuffing all that and

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.560
more into 81 minutes of tooth-gritted cheer.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.820
It's not just that stuff that makes this take
on Dickens so skippable.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.180
Despite the A-list talent, Christmas Carol:
The Movie is remarkably lifeless.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.760
The facial animation doesn't ever look quite
right, the acting is, at best, stiff, and

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.320
the entire operation feels dumbed down.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.100
This adaptation's fatal flaw is that it doesn't
trust Dickens, and it doesn't trust its audience.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.280
This 2003 TV movie attempts to liven up the
classic tale by replacing Scrooge with a selfish

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.540
talk show host and moving the story from Victorian
London to modern-day Hollywood.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.700
It's an intriguing idea on its own.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.380
What setting better represents a story of
greed, empathy, and false fronts than Hollywood?

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.480
And hey, isn't the idea of Scrooge as an image-obsessed
celebrity pretty great?

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.640
In concept, it's all strong and innovative.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.500
Too bad the execution isn't.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:30.110
A Carol Christmas is little more than a fresh
coat of paint that flakes away over the course

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.640
of the movie, revealing that it wasn't all
that necessary to begin with.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.360
The modern setting makes Dickens' story more
cloying than anything else.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.180
Removed from sooty London, the sickly children
and terrifying visions of the future feel

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.860
more like after-school specials than grave
warnings.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.800
Worst of all, A Carol Christmas is simply
boring.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.020
By the end the audience isn't so much watching
the movie as it is waiting it out.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.970
The cranky celebrity might learn to be kinder
by the end, but the viewer has only learned

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.280
to distrust new takes on old favorites.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.620
There was a sliver of time in the 2000s when
movies fell in love with surreal, motion-capture-powered

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.970
computer animation.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:09.369
Robert Zemeckis, fresh from using this technique
in The Polar Express and Beowulf, decided

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:11.120
to apply it to A Christmas Carol.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.340
Led by performances from Jim Carrey as Scrooge
and Gary Oldman as Bob Cratchit, the results

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.480
were...odd.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.840
Some, like Roger Ebert, hailed it as "an exhilarating
visual experience."

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.340
Others, like The Telegraph, considered its
looks distracting, creating distance between

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.140
the characters and the audience that made
it difficult to connect to the story.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.060
"There's more of gravy than of grave about
you, whatever you are."

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.260
"WAAAAH!"

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.600
Years later, the movie's reputation remains
mixed, and it's not hard to see why.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.480
There are certainly strengths to be found.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.700
Carrey's Scrooge is a bit goofier than other
renditions, but it fits the ghoulishly funny

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.980
tone of the movie, and Oldman's Cratchit is
pitch-perfect.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.200
But the visuals, so dazzling in 2009, look
strange to modern eyes.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.630
Plus, the tone of the film sits uncomfortably
between two extremes.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.880
It's a little too dark to be heartwarming
and a bit too silly to be bleak.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.880
Only one thing is truly clear: It's nobody's
go-to version when the holiday season rolls around.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.740
You might not recognize Alan Menken by name,
but there's a good chance you know his work.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:15.620
The Little Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast,
and Tangled all feature his songs.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.080
So when he signed on to a brand new adaptation
of A Christmas Carol starring Kelsey Grammer,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.040
hopes were high.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.660
The man behind "A Whole New World" and "Under the Sea"

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.520
tackling one of the greatest holiday stories ever told?

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.280
That's not just a good idea — it's about as safe a bet as anyone gets in the entertainment industry.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.960
And yet somehow, this version of Scrooge's
fateful transformation

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.520
leaves something to be desired.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.240
The music is quite good, though it never hits
the highs of Menken's greatest work.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.980
The story is solidly told, and the movie features
a fine performance from Jane Krakowski as

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.810
the Ghost of Christmas Past.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.320
Grammer is as grumpy as Scrooge should be,
without ever crossing the line into total

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.180
awfulness.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.980
And yet the entire affair is somehow inert.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.500
It never stops feeling like something adapted
from the stage that probably should've stayed there.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.640
On film, it never offends, but it doesn't
impress either.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.920
It's trapped between worlds, unable to succeed
in either one.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.740
Disney has been involved in multiple Christmas
Carol adaptations, but this is the one that

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.340
features the characters most central to the
brand.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:17.100
Mickey, Minnie, and of course, Scrooge McDuck
all star in this particular take on the tale.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.109
They bring a certain lightness to even the
darkest parts of the iconic story.

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:25.200
No one heads into A Christmas Carol thinking
Scrooge won't end up changing his ways, but

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.420
there's still something soothing about Mickey
Mouse leading the bleak first half of the story.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.360
For the youngest viewers, this is an ideal
Carol indeed.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.960
"Farewell, Ebenezer…"

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.000
Unfortunately, that's the only group this
version is meant for.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.040
Anyone beyond the preschool years won't find
much to love about this take, blunted as it

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.940
is by Disney's core characters.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.900
This isn't to say it isn't sweet to watch
beloved figures like Daisy Duck and Goofy

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.580
in London, learning the ways of goodwill toward
men.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:58.840
The sheer number of A Christmas Carol adaptations,
though, means that no viewer has to settle

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.080
for mere sweetness.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.860
Leave this version in the nursery where it
belongs, and move on to something better.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:09.320
Unique among Carol adaptations, Scrooged actually
features an in-universe staging of the classic

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.280
Dickens tale.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.699
Shrewd TV executive Frank Cross ordered it,
and he doesn't care how much strain it puts

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:15.910
on his overworked employees.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.600
He is, of course, the Scrooge of this story.

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.640
"Oh my Gosh, does that suck."

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.880
He's even got an overworked assistant, a widow
named Grace, and an old flame whose work with

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.380
the homeless contrasts sharply with his greed.

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:35.100
Three ghosts visit him, lessons are learned,
and in the end, Frank, leads the Carol crew

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:39.040
in a rendition of the 1969 classic "Put a
Little Love in Your Heart."

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.420
Spins on Carol can go quite well, and this
one does, for the most part.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.740
Modern mass media suits the protagonist, and
it's different enough from the original story

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.080
to be intriguing but similar enough to translate
smoothly.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.960
Bill Murray in the title role plays Cross
with sharky, calculating menace.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.920
He's a Scrooge the audience fears on a primal
level, as he's so destructive in a familiar way.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.960
But there is a mean-spirited tone to the film,
as though the movie is embarrassed to seem

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:03.740
too earnest.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.780
This pairs oddly with the sentimental ending,
which actually goes further down the road

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.940
to a happy ending than most Carols.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.180
All in all, it's fun...but only just.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.500
This isn't the only animated rendition of
A Christmas Carol on our list, but it might

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:20.790
just be the most visually striking, so much
so that it won an Oscar for Best Animated

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:21.680
Short Film.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.860
Richard Williams, who masterminded Who Framed
Roger Rabbit?, directed and produced this

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.780
version, and the quality is clear in every
frame.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:33.180
This version of A Christmas Carol is austere,
almost dour, thanks to the crew looking to

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.660
19th-century engravings for inspiration.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.020
As a result, this version might not be the
one children respond to.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.900
Plus, it's a short piece, only 25 minutes
long.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:45.920
You can't help but wish the film had a little
more room to breathe, if only to explore the

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.360
possibilities of its unique animation more.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.830
Still, the end result is a gorgeous half-hour
of pure Dickens, as warm-hearted as it is

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:54.580
deliberately rough around the edges.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:58.550
You forget the happy ending within the grimy
first half of the tale, so it's all the more

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.840
delightful to rediscover that in the second.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:04.820
Dickens' work isn't exactly what anyone would
describe as "feel good."

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:08.640
Sure, A Christmas Carol has a happy ending,
but the reader needs to earn it.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.980
"Dickensian," after all, has become a descriptor
unto itself.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:16.260
And this version of A Christmas Carol (aka
Scrooge) is truly Dickensian.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:21.620
British giant of the stage and screen Alastair
Sim dons the stovepipe hat in this adaptation,

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.200
and he cranks up the grumpiness to memorable
levels.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:28.620
It's a notably grim take on an already dark
tale, but it works, for the most part due

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:30.940
to Sim's unbeatable screen presence.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.120
You hate Scrooge, but you never give up on
him.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:37.560
That stirring central performance livens up
the whole production, making the film actively

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.280
entertaining, instead of simply faithful to
Dickens.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:44.640
This is a deeply honest Christmas Carol, for
better and worse, but it never loses its faith

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.480
in humanity's ability to change.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.840
A version of A Christmas Carol starring Patrick
Stewart already has a leg up on the competition.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:55.740
Those who know his work will automatically
see him as a natural Scrooge, while those

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:58.040
who are new to his work will be quickly convinced.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:02.200
His gruffness suits the character perfectly,
while his comedy chops come in handy towards

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:02.700
the end.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.980
Stewart is an actor at the height of his powers
in this adaptation, as natural rolling his

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:09.680
eyes at orphans as he is embracing Tiny Tim.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:13.820
He gives the entire production gravity, and
he loans every scene and set piece weight

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.920
and sophistication with his presence.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:20.700
This take A Christmas Carol helps you understand
how Stewart's career has, if anything, only

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:21.760
grown with age.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:24.560
The movie around him, in contrast, is merely
fine.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.280
There's nothing to complain about, but beyond
Stewart's powerhouse performance, nothing

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:29.360
catches the eye.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.560
That's easily forgiven thanks to Stewart,
but when placed beside other versions of the

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:35.440
story, it's a lot more noticeable.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.380
Stewart is talented enough to make the production
notable, but in truth, he's the only thing

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:40.080
that does.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:45.320
Happily for Dickens and Stewart fans alike,
A Christmas Carol is very much Ebenezer Scrooge's story.

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:48.200
Thus, this time, it's Patrick Stewart's story
as well.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:52.840
"What the dickens have they done to Scrooge?"
the tagline of the Scrooge poster pleads.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.000
It's also a musical, and one made in 1970
at that.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.140
You hear these facts and imagine a sort of
perfect, anti-Dickens storm approaching.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:02.880
And yet, Scrooge actually ends up being pretty
delightful.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.269
Yes, it enjoys thumbing its nose at ideas
of the Victorian era and what a literary adaptation

00:09:08.269 --> 00:09:08.920
should be.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.000
But its heart is fixed firmly in the right
place, and that results in a truly wonderful

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.819
rendition of A Christmas Carol.

00:09:14.819 --> 00:09:19.360
The songs serve the story rather than smothering
it, clarifying Dickens' themes as they spin

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.200
them into an entirely new medium.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.220
Is this jauntier than the more classical takes
on the story?

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:25.800
Yes, but that's a virtue.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.060
Scrooge might be making its own rules, but
it never loses sight of its source material.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:35.340
There's no shortage of Christmas Carol adaptations
featuring top-class talent, especially in

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.320
the role of Scrooge.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.580
But George C. Scott brings something unmatched
to this version, a sheer power that pours

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:41.820
from every frame.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.980
An actor from West Virginia brings something
to a role as essentially British as Ebenezer

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.200
Scrooge that the actual Brits could do well
to learn from him.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:53.480
This is a Scrooge who strides, and whose nastiness
stings all the more for the force he can put

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:54.360
behind it.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:59.459
Thus, it's also a Scrooge who feels entirely
transformed by the final credits, and a Scrooge

00:09:59.459 --> 00:10:01.420
who delivers the moral with renewed energy.

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:05.380
He isn't just changed by his ordeal with the
ghosts, but rebuilt as a new man with new

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.220
purpose.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:11.040
Alongside a cast that brings a certain worn
quality many Carol adaptations lack, Scott

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.400
shines all the brighter.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.880
It's not just a great take on a classic story,
or a collection of impressive performances.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.920
It's a holiday treasure all its own.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.960
Who'd have thought that a rendition of A Christmas
Carol starring Muppets would be one of the

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:24.720
best adaptations of all?

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.460
Unlike the worst offenders on this list, this
film doesn't just trot out the morals everyone expects.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.620
It also doesn't put any unnecessary new spin
on things that don't need changing.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.240
Rather, it highlights the best things about
the story while providing a new lens with

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.440
which to view them.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:41.760
Thus, it helps us love them like we did the
very first time we heard the story.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.040
To start with, there's Michael Caine's Scrooge.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:48.700
He's as grouchy as he should be, but surrounding
him with puppets teases out a comical edge.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.280
He's a terror, but you're laughing at him
from the beginning.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.380
This makes the movie an ideal choice for the
youngest viewers, but it also gives the film

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.200
an inventive spin on Dickens' tale.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:01.060
While some on this list succeed by leaning
into the story's darker elements, the Muppets

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:04.900
succeed by embracing the author's sense of
humor, joy, and goodwill.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:09.240
Plus, you'll get to see Gonzo, best known
for his weird blue nose and love of chickens,

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.700
play cultural cornerstone Charles Dickens.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:12.700
What's not to love?

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.000
What makes A Christmas Carol great?

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.120
There's the satisfaction of seeing a villain
confronted with the consequences of his actions.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.940
There's the sly sense of humor that peeks
out from around the story's most cynical corners.

00:11:22.949 --> 00:11:25.509
Plus, there's the community spirit that it
celebrates.

00:11:25.509 --> 00:11:27.839
But beyond all of that, there's the hope it
kindles.

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:31.880
A Christmas Carol tells us that it's never
too late to be a bigger, better, and more

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.019
benevolent person.

00:11:33.019 --> 00:11:34.970
"I shall change my way of living.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.339
I will try to keep Christmas all the year."

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.540
It's this spirit that the 1938 adaptation
embodies.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:46.119
From its first scenes to its sublime ending,
it's a beacon of progress in all its forms.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:50.060
Reginald Owen's Scrooge is an old monster
who finds himself falling in love with life

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.780
again, flush with the fact that it isn't too
late for him.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:56.900
That this film was made in the midst of the
Great Depression for a weary public remains clear.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:01.260
This is a movie for those all too familiar
with Scrooge and his ilk, as Dickens' audience

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:02.220
was in its day.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:06.780
This version of A Christmas Carol understands
the power of its story like few others, and

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:10.880
decades after its debut, it still shines like
it did the day of its premiere.

